{:edb/db-before-t 1545 :edb/db-after-t 1546 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534858 50 #inst "2026-10-08T09:16:26.003Z" 13194139534858 true] [17592186091893 1100 "Which of the following expressions are polynomials?\n\n- $(5x^2-10x^3) \\div (5x)$\n\n- $(1-x^3) \\div (x^3)$\n\n- $(2x^5) \\cdot (-x^3-7)$" 13194139534858 true] [17592186091893 1100 "Which of the following expressions are polynomials?\n\n1. $(5x^2-10x^3) \\div (5x)$\n2. $(1-x^3) \\div (x^3)$\n3. $(2x^5) \\cdot (-x^3-7)$" 13194139534858 false] [17592186091893 1145 "Recall that polynomials have the following closure properties:\n\n- Polynomials are closed under addition, subtraction, and multiplication. This means that the sum, difference, and product of two polynomials is *always* a polynomial.\n\n- Polynomials are *not* closed under division. This means that the quotient of two polynomials is *not* always a polynomial.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our expressions in turn.\n\n- Expression I is a polynomial. Even though polynomials are *not* closed under division, the quotient of these two polynomials results in a polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5{x}^{2}-10{x}^{3})÷(5x) & =\\frac{5{x}^{2}-10{x}^{3}}{5x} \\\\  & =\\frac{5x(x-2{x}^{2})}{5x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{5x}(x-2{x}^{2})}{\\cancel{5x}} \\\\  & =x-2{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Expression II is *not* a polynomial. Carrying out the division, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}} & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* a polynomial.\n\n- Expression III is a polynomial. It's a product of two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139534858 true] [17592186091893 1145 "Recall that polynomials have the following closure properties:\n\n- Polynomials are closed under addition, subtraction, and multiplication. This means that the sum, difference, and product of two polynomials is *always* a polynomial.\n- Polynomials are *not* closed under division. This means that the quotient of two polynomials is *not* always a polynomial.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our expressions in turn.\n\n- Expression I is a polynomial. Even though polynomials are *not* closed under division, the quotient of these two polynomials results in a polynomial:\n$(5x^{2} - 10x^{3}) \\div (5x)|= \\frac{5x^{2} - 10x^{3}}{5x}; = (5x(x - 2x^{2}))/(5x); = (5x(x - 2x^{2}))/(5x); = x - 2x^{2}$\n- Expression II is *not* a polynomial. Carrying out the division, we get\n$\\frac{1 - x^{3}}{x^{3}}|= \\frac{1}{x^{3}} - \\frac{x^{3}}{x^{3}}; = \\frac{1}{x^{3}} - 1$\nwhich is *not* a polynomial.\n- Expression III is a polynomial. It's a product of two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139534858 false] [17592186091894 1100 "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $3$ and $2$, respectively, what is the degree of $P - Q$?" 13194139534858 false] [17592186091894 1100 "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $3$ and $2$, respectively, what is the degree of $P - Q?$" 13194139534858 true] [17592186091894 1145 "Since $P$ is a polynomial of degree $3$, the leading term contains an $x^3$.\n\nSince $Q$ is a polynomial of degree $2$, the leading term contains an $x^2$.\n\nIn $P-Q$, the highest-exponent term still contains an $x^3$. There is no $x^3$ term in $Q$ that could possibly cancel out the $x^3$ term in $P$.\n\nTherefore, the degree of $P-Q$ must be $3$." 13194139534858 false] [17592186091894 1145 "Since $P$ is a polynomial of degree $3,$ the leading term contains an $x^3.$\n\nSince $Q$ is a polynomial of degree $2,$ the leading term contains an $x^2.$\n\nIn $P-Q,$ the highest-exponent term still contains an $x^3.$ There is no $x^3$ term in $Q$ that could possibly cancel out the $x^3$ term in $P.$\n\nTherefore, the degree of $P-Q$ must be $3.$" 13194139534858 true] [17592186091895 1100 "Given that $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $5$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P \\cdot Q$ is always a polynomial of degree $10$,\n\n- $P + Q$ is always a polynomial of degree $5$,\n\n- $\\dfrac{P}{Q}$ is sometimes a polynomial." 13194139534858 true] [17592186091895 1100 "Given that $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $5$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $P \\cdot Q$ is always a polynomial of degree $10$,\n2. $P + Q$ is always a polynomial of degree $5$,\n3. $\\dfrac{P}{Q}$ is sometimes a polynomial." 13194139534858 false] [17592186091895 1145 "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The highest-degree terms in $P$ and $Q$ must both contain $x^5$. Therefore, the highest-degree term in $P \\cdot Q$ must contain $x^5 \\cdot x^5 = x^{5+5} = x^{10}$, meaning that $P \\cdot Q$ has degree $10$.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, suppose that $P=x^5$ and $Q=-x^5$. Then $P+Q = x^5+(-x^5)=0$, which is not a polynomial of degree $5$.\n- Statement III is true. For example, suppose that $P=x^5$ and $Q=x^5$. Then $\\dfrac{P}{Q} = \\dfrac{x^5}{x^5} = 1$, which is a polynomial of degree $0$.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and III are true." 13194139534858 false] [17592186091895 1145 "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The highest-degree terms in $P$ and $Q$ must both contain $x^5.$ Therefore, the highest-degree term in $P \\cdot Q$ must contain $x^5 \\cdot x^5 = x^{5+5} = x^{10},$ meaning that $P \\cdot Q$ has degree $10.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, suppose that $P=x^5$ and $Q=-x^5.$ Then $P+Q = x^5+(-x^5)=0,$ which is not a polynomial of degree $5.$\n\n- Statement III is true. For example, suppose that $P=x^5$ and $Q=x^5.$ Then $\\dfrac{P}{Q} = \\dfrac{x^5}{x^5} = 1,$ which is a polynomial of degree $0.$\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and III are true." 13194139534858 true] [17592186092106 1100 "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $2$ and $8$, respectively, what is the degree of $P \\cdot Q$?" 13194139534858 false] [17592186092106 1100 "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $2$ and $8$, respectively, what is the degree of $P \\cdot Q?$" 13194139534858 true] [17592186092106 1145 "Since $P$ is a polynomial of degree $2$, the leading term contains an $x^2$.\n\nSince $Q$ is a polynomial of degree $8$, the leading term contains an $x^8$.\n\nIn $P \\cdot Q$, the $x^2$ term in $P$ multiplies with the $x^8$ term in $Q$ to form a term containing\n\n$$\nx^2 \\cdot x^8 = x^{2+8} = x^{10}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the degree of $P \\cdot Q$ must be $10$." 13194139534858 false] [17592186092106 1145 "Since $P$ is a polynomial of degree $2,$ the leading term contains an $x^2.$\n\nSince $Q$ is a polynomial of degree $8,$ the leading term contains an $x^8.$\n\nIn $P \\cdot Q,$ the $x^2$ term in $P$ multiplies with the $x^8$ term in $Q$ to form a term containing $x^2 \\cdot x^8 = x^{2+8} = x^{10}.$\n\nTherefore, the degree of $P \\cdot Q$ must be $10.$" 13194139534858 true] [17592186092259 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315448 13194139534858 true] [17592186092259 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315455 13194139534858 true] [17592186092259 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315461 13194139534858 true] [17592186092259 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315464 13194139534858 true] [17592186092259 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315478 13194139534858 true] [17592186092274 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315420 13194139534858 true] [17592186092274 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315427 13194139534858 true] [17592186092274 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315434 13194139534858 true] [17592186092274 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315441 13194139534858 true] [17592186092275 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315458 13194139534858 true] [17592186092275 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315468 13194139534858 true] [17592186092275 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315471 13194139534858 true] [17592186092275 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315485 13194139534858 true] [17592186092275 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315492 13194139534858 true] [17592186092276 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315406 13194139534858 true] [17592186092276 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315413 13194139534858 true] [17592186092276 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315499 13194139534858 true] [17592186092499 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534858 true] [17592186092499 1100 "Which of the following expressions are polynomials?\n\n- $({x}^{2}+4x)-(-5{x}^{3}+3)$\n- $(2-{x}^{3})÷({x}^{2})$\n- $(2x-{x}^{3})\\cdot ({x}^{2}-x+1)$" 13194139534858 true] [17592186092499 1100 "Which of the following expressions are polynomials?\n\n1. $(x^{2} + 4x) - (-5x^{3} + 3)$\n2. $(2 - x^{3}) \\div (x^{2})$\n3. $(2x - x^{3}) \\cdot (x^{2} - x + 1)$" 13194139534858 false] [17592186092499 1145 "Recall that polynomials have the following closure properties:\n\n- Polynomials are closed under addition, subtraction, and multiplication. This means that the sum, difference, and product of two polynomials is *always* a polynomial.\n\n- Polynomials are *not* closed under division. This means that the quotient of two polynomials is *not* always a polynomial.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our expressions in turn.\n\n- Expression I is a polynomial. It's a difference between two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under subtraction.\n\n- Expression II is *not* a polynomial. Carrying out the division, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2-{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}-x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* a polynomial.\n\n- Expression III gives a polynomial. It's a product of two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139534858 true] [17592186092661 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534858 true] [17592186092661 1100 "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $2$ and $6$, respectively, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $P + Q$ is sometimes a polynomial of degree $4$\n2. $P - Q$ is always a polynomial\n3. $\\frac{P}{Q}$ is always a polynomial" 13194139534858 false] [17592186092661 1100 "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $2$ and $6,$ respectively, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P+Q$ is sometimes a polynomial of degree $4$\n- $P-Q$ is always a polynomial\n- $\\frac{P}{Q}$ is always a polynomial" 13194139534858 true] [17592186092661 1145 "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The highest-degree terms in $P$ and $Q$ must contain ${x}^{2}$ and ${x}^{6},$ respectively. In $P+Q,$ the leading term still contains an ${x}^{6}.$ There is no ${x}^{6}$ term in $P$ that could possibly cancel the ${x}^{6}$ term in $Q.$ Therefore, the degree of $P+Q$ must always be $6.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Polynomials are closed under subtraction.\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, suppose $P={x}^{2}$ and $Q={x}^{6}.$ Then $\\frac{P}{Q}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{6}}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}$ which is not a polynomial.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement II is true." 13194139534858 true] [17592186315406 1017 #edb/ref 17592186315407 13194139534858 true] [17592186315406 1089 "q-111385" 13194139534858 true] [17592186315406 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534858 true] [17592186315406 1100 "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $2$ and $4,$ respectively, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P+Q$ is always a polynomial\n- $P-Q$ is sometimes a polynomial of degree $3$\n- $\\frac{P}{Q}$ is sometimes a polynomial" 13194139534858 true] [17592186315406 1113 #uuid "d1492386-6d0c-51d6-9c6a-e6f64d3cb61e" 13194139534858 true] [17592186315406 1145 "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Polynomials are closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. The highest-degree terms in $P$ and $Q$ must contain ${x}^{2}$ and ${x}^{4},$ respectively. Since there is no ${x}^{4}$ term in $P$ that could cancel the ${x}^{4}$ term in $Q,$ the leading term in $P-Q$  still contains ${x}^{4}.$  Therefore, the degree of $P-Q$ must always be $4.$\n\n- Statement III is false. For the cases where $\\frac{P}{Q}$ is a polynomial, we need that the degree of $P$ should be greater or equal to the degree of $Q,$ which is not the case.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is true." 13194139534858 true] [17592186315407 1000 #edb/ref 17592186315408 13194139534858 true] [17592186315407 1000 #edb/ref 17592186315409 13194139534858 true] [17592186315407 1000 #edb/ref 17592186315410 13194139534858 true] [17592186315407 1000 #edb/ref 17592186315411 13194139534858 true] [17592186315407 1000 #edb/ref 17592186315412 13194139534858 true] [17592186315407 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534858 true] [17592186315407 1041 "selection" 13194139534858 true] [17592186315407 1104 #edb/ref 17592186315410 13194139534858 true] [17592186315407 1110 #uuid "c81186fb-2a3d-5ae5-865f-8b1dc7654013" 13194139534858 true] [17592186315408 1034 "II and III only" 13194139534858 true] [17592186315408 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534858 true] [17592186315408 1111 #uuid "1fcf11d6-572f-57e3-af69-334511c72244" 13194139534858 true] [17592186315409 1034 "I and III only" 13194139534858 true] [17592186315409 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534858 true] [17592186315409 1111 #uuid "2260fcd5-3374-554d-abee-7b008df8f628" 13194139534858 true] [17592186315410 1034 "I only" 13194139534858 true] [17592186315410 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534858 true] [17592186315410 1111 #uuid "e398e850-417f-580c-88b4-456922585e7f" 13194139534858 true] [17592186315411 1034 "III only" 13194139534858 true] [17592186315411 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534858 true] [17592186315411 1111 #uuid "7de7608d-fb39-5c91-8866-00bc20a344fe" 13194139534858 true] [17592186315412 1034 "II only" 13194139534858 true] [17592186315412 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534858 true] [17592186315412 1111 #uuid "b6f6e3b6-2354-58ad-bb9c-fb06da5e613e" 13194139534858 true] [17592186315413 1017 #edb/ref 17592186315414 13194139534858 true] [17592186315413 1089 "q-111390" 13194139534858 true] [17592186315413 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534858 true] [17592186315413 1100 "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $2,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P\\cdot Q$ is a polynomial of degree $4$\n- $P-Q$ is sometimes a polynomial of degree $1$\n- $P+Q$ is always a polynomial of degree $2$" 13194139534858 true] [17592186315413 1113 #uuid "fc10624a-1d97-52d8-b614-80cd282f5aeb" 13194139534858 true] [17592186315413 1145 "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The highest-degree terms in $P$ and $Q$ must both contain ${x}^{2}.$ Therefore, the highest-degree term in $P\\cdot Q$ must contain ${x}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{2}={x}^{2+2}={x}^{4},$ meaning that $P\\cdot Q$ has degree $4.$\n\n- Statement II is true. For example, suppose that $P={x}^{2}+x$ and $Q={x}^{2}.$ Then, $P-Q={x}^{2}+x-{x}^{2}=x,$ which is a polynomial of degree $1.$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, suppose $P={x}^{2}$ and $Q=-{x}^{2}.$ Then, $P+Q={x}^{2}+(-{x}^{2})=0,$ which is not a polynomial of degree $2.$\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and II are true." 13194139534858 true] [17592186315414 1000 #edb/ref 17592186315415 13194139534858 true] [17592186315414 1000 #edb/ref 17592186315416 13194139534858 true] [17592186315414 1000 #edb/ref 17592186315417 13194139534858 true] [17592186315414 1000 #edb/ref 17592186315418 13194139534858 true] [17592186315414 1000 #edb/ref 17592186315419 13194139534858 true] [17592186315414 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534858 true] [17592186315414 1041 "selection" 13194139534858 true] [17592186315414 1104 #edb/ref 17592186315418 13194139534858 true] [17592186315414 1110 #uuid "a814c045-d35a-5bdb-aa7a-ae5c09ccd3d9" 13194139534858 true] [17592186315415 1034 "II and III only" 13194139534858 true] [17592186315415 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534858 true] [17592186315415 1111 #uuid "771d660b-ae72-53ff-aa82-80c0a3243a6b" 13194139534858 true] [17592186315416 1034 "I and III only" 13194139534858 true] [17592186315416 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534858 true] [17592186315416 1111 #uuid "712e9ae8-b93b-533d-9fba-c670a1ce55fe" 13194139534858 true] [17592186315417 1034 "I only" 13194139534858 true] [17592186315417 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534858 true] [17592186315417 1111 #uuid "8c79a1f6-1faa-5d64-8906-f514e2654892" 13194139534858 true] [17592186315418 1034 "I and II only" 13194139534858 true] [17592186315418 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534858 true] [17592186315418 1111 #uuid "4522e59e-0963-5636-a5b9-67b82da1f4b9" 13194139534858 true] [17592186315419 1034 "II only" 13194139534858 true] [17592186315419 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534858 true] [17592186315419 1111 #uuid "c62eec66-2f1f-574f-a9ef-a8145c8d3d91" 13194139534858 true] [17592186315420 1017 #edb/ref 17592186315421 13194139534858 true] [17592186315420 1089 "q-141234" 13194139534858 true] [17592186315420 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534858 true] [17592186315420 1100 "Which of the following expressions are polynomials?\n\n- $(x)+(-{x}^{3}-1)$\n- $(2{x}^{4}-3)÷({x}^{2})$\n- $({x}^{2})-(2{x}^{5}+4x)$" 13194139534858 true] [17592186315420 1113 #uuid "43473837-68b6-5be4-987f-f091e37ac03d" 13194139534858 true] [17592186315420 1145 "Recall that polynomials have the following closure properties:\n\n- Polynomials are closed under addition, subtraction, and multiplication. This means that the sum, difference, and product of two polynomials is *always* a polynomial.\n\n- Polynomials are *not* closed under division. This means that the quotient of two polynomials is *not* always a polynomial.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our expressions in turn.\n\n- Expression I is a polynomial. It's a sum of two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under addition.\n\n- Expression II is *not* a polynomial. Carrying out the division, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{x}^{4}-3}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{2{x}^{4}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* a polynomial.\n\n- Expression III gives a polynomial. It's a difference between two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under subtraction.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139534858 true] [17592186315421 1000 #edb/ref 17592186315422 13194139534858 true] [17592186315421 1000 #edb/ref 17592186315423 13194139534858 true] [17592186315421 1000 #edb/ref 17592186315424 13194139534858 true] [17592186315421 1000 #edb/ref 17592186315425 13194139534858 true] [17592186315421 1000 #edb/ref 17592186315426 13194139534858 true] [17592186315421 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534858 true] [17592186315421 1041 "selection" 13194139534858 true] [17592186315421 1104 #edb/ref 17592186315424 13194139534858 true] [17592186315421 1110 #uuid "6d48d013-a62b-561f-be1d-f3b4767c0f96" 13194139534858 true] [17592186315422 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139534858 true] [17592186315422 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534858 true] [17592186315422 1111 #uuid "b59fdc75-fd8c-5168-a0c6-2aa8dae53efc" 13194139534858 true] [17592186315423 1034 "II and III only" 13194139534858 true] [17592186315423 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534858 true] [17592186315423 1111 #uuid "9cc11679-aabd-5a6b-81fa-9bc49d982f32" 13194139534858 true] [17592186315424 1034 "I and III only" 13194139534858 true] [17592186315424 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534858 true] [17592186315424 1111 #uuid "5f3c881e-b7a4-594b-aa34-899a8c060404" 13194139534858 true] [17592186315425 1034 "I only" 13194139534858 true] [17592186315425 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534858 true] [17592186315425 1111 #uuid "a1a4e71d-cde9-5e2d-b051-5e10b92f54b1" 13194139534858 true] [17592186315426 1034 "III only" 13194139534858 true] [17592186315426 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534858 true] [17592186315426 1111 #uuid "63824682-6cd1-5d8e-b03d-af409b2c83d3" 13194139534858 true] [17592186315427 1017 #edb/ref 17592186315428 13194139534858 true] [17592186315427 1089 "q-141236" 13194139534858 true] [17592186315427 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534858 true] [17592186315427 1100 "Which of the following expressions are polynomials?\n\n- $(4{x}^{6}-2)÷(2x)$\n- $(7x+1)\\cdot (2{x}^{3}+3)$\n- $({x}^{4}-3{x}^{2}+1)÷({x}^{2})$" 13194139534858 true] [17592186315427 1113 #uuid "a0455dfc-71d9-5697-8db9-62e19eeb0c14" 13194139534858 true] [17592186315427 1145 "Recall that polynomials have the following closure properties:\n\n- Polynomials are closed under addition, subtraction, and multiplication. This means that the sum, difference, and product of two polynomials is *always* a polynomial.\n\n- Polynomials are *not* closed under division. This means that the quotient of two polynomials is *not* always a polynomial.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our expressions in turn.\n\n- Expression I is *not* a polynomial. Carrying out the division, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4{x}^{6}-2}{2x} & =\\frac{4{x}^{6}}{2x}-\\frac{2}{2x} \\\\  & =2{x}^{5}-\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* a polynomial.\n\n- Expression II gives a polynomial. It's a product of two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under multiplication.\n\n- Expression III is *not* a polynomial. Carrying out the division, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{4}-3{x}^{2}+1}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-3+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* a polynomial.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139534858 true] [17592186315428 1000 #edb/ref 17592186315429 13194139534858 true] [17592186315428 1000 #edb/ref 17592186315430 13194139534858 true] [17592186315428 1000 #edb/ref 17592186315431 13194139534858 true] [17592186315428 1000 #edb/ref 17592186315432 13194139534858 true] [17592186315428 1000 #edb/ref 17592186315433 13194139534858 true] [17592186315428 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534858 true] [17592186315428 1041 "selection" 13194139534858 true] [17592186315428 1104 #edb/r